1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,367
One bad step.

2
00:00:01,367 --> 00:00:02,300
A crack.

3
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,867
Your leg will not hold you.

4
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:10,700
Now put that moment thirty thousand years in the past. No
ambulance. No hospital.

5
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,267
And the sun is going down.

6
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:15,033
Game over? Not necessarily.

7
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:20,467
Archaeologists have found a person who survived a
devastating leg injury for years.

8
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:24,267
Some researchers think the damaged limb was deliberately
amputated.

9
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:30,933
We will get to that remarkable case. First, our imaginary
traveller has a more immediate problem.

10
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:38,400
The shelter is over there. He is over here. And the space
between them has suddenly become enormous.

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:44,233
He checks the leg again. Still broken. This is going to
complicate the afternoon.

12
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:50,067
A few minutes ago, that slope was a shortcut. Now it is a
wall.

13
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:57,133
The stream was somewhere to drink. Now it is an obstacle.
Nothing in the landscape has changed.

14
00:00:57,133 --> 00:00:59,700
Your ability to cross it has.

15
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,467
Our traveller is fictional.

16
00:01:01,467 --> 00:01:08,133
The problem is real: someone has to notice that you are
missing before they can help.

17
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:15,200
In our story, two companions come back. One hears the
shouting. The other sees the abandoned basket.

18
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:23,100
They are not arriving with a magic cure. They are arriving
with time, effort, and another pair of hands.

19
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:29,767
Eventually, our traveller makes it back to shelter with
help. The landscape has not become easier.

20
00:01:29,767 --> 00:01:33,567
He simply no longer has to cross it alone.

21
00:01:33,567 --> 00:01:41,467
For one glorious moment, being back at camp feels like the
end of the problem. Then you look down.

22
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:44,467
The leg has come home with you.

23
00:01:44,467 --> 00:01:47,867
A broken leg is not one single injury.

24
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:54,133
A relatively stable fracture and a badly displaced break do
not create the same problems.

25
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:56,700
Damage to surrounding tissue matters too.

26
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,900
If the skin is broken, germs can enter the wound.

27
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,100
Serious fractures can also threaten nearby blood vessels and
nerves.

28
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:10,133
Without modern treatment, those complications could make a
bad situation much worse.

29
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:14,333
And even an injury that heals does not heal overnight.

30
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:22,233
Today, the NHS describes six to twelve weeks as a usual
healing range for a broken leg, sometimes longer.

31
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:26,433
That is a modern comparison, not an ancient recovery
schedule.

32
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:33,500
Our traveller was hoping for one uncomfortable evening. He
is now looking at a much longer arrangement.

33
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:37,700
Tomorrow he needs food. The next day he needs food.

34
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:42,333
His body has absolutely no interest in how inconvenient this
is.

35
00:02:42,333 --> 00:02:46,967
Which means the next threat might not look dramatic at all.

36
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:53,633
It might look like an empty place where your share of the
work used to be.

37
00:02:53,633 --> 00:03:01,100
Imagine the jobs that still need doing around this camp.
Gathering fuel. Finding food. Carrying supplies. Maintaining
shelter.

38
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:06,933
The injury does not cancel any of them. It changes who can
do them.

39
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:14,400
Someone bringing you food is spending time they could spend
elsewhere. Someone staying nearby may miss another task.

40
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:20,233
Helping an injured person can be an ongoing commitment, not
a single heroic gesture.

41
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:23,633
But needing help does not make someone useless.

42
00:03:23,633 --> 00:03:33,967
In our imaginary camp, the traveller can still share what he
knows, watch what is happening, or work with his hands when
he is able.

43
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:35,733
His role can change.

44
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:39,533
We should not pretend every ancient group behaved alike.

45
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:46,200
Researchers studying Neanderthals argue that care for
injured people could make an important contribution to
survival.

46
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:50,833
The evidence includes injuries that healed while the person
continued living.

47
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:55,033
That is a stronger clue than any scene we invent.

48
00:03:55,033 --> 00:04:01,700
So let us leave our traveller by the fire and meet someone
whose bones actually survived.

49
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:09,600
At Shanidar Cave, in what is now Iraq, archaeologists found
the remains of a Neanderthal known as Shanidar One.

50
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:17,067
His case takes us beyond an imagined accident and into a
body that carried its injuries for years.

51
00:04:17,067 --> 00:04:19,633
His skeleton records several serious injuries.

52
00:04:19,633 --> 00:04:27,933
Researchers describe damage around the left eye, a severely
impaired right arm, and a healed fracture in the right foot.

53
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:33,767
Yet he lived into adulthood, with age at death estimated
around thirty-five to forty-five.

54
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:41,667
That does not tell us every treatment he received. It does
not let us name the people who helped.

55
00:04:41,667 --> 00:04:46,700
It does show that the injuries were not all immediate death
sentences.

56
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:54,167
His survival is widely interpreted as evidence of support
from others. The skeleton gives us injuries and healing.

57
00:04:54,167 --> 00:04:58,800
The daily work of care has to be reconstructed more
cautiously.

58
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:05,067
Still, the cartoon of prehistory where everyone simply
abandons the injured is already looking shaky.

59
00:05:05,067 --> 00:05:11,333
And another discovery takes the question further. What if
part of a leg was gone?

60
00:05:11,333 --> 00:05:18,800
In Liang Tebo cave, on the island of Borneo, researchers
uncovered a burial about thirty-one thousand years old.

61
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:25,467
The young adult inside was missing the left foot and part of
the lower left leg.

62
00:05:25,467 --> 00:05:30,900
Missing bones alone would not prove surgery. Bodies can be
disturbed after burial.

63
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:36,333
Here, the researchers examined something more revealing:
changes in the surviving leg bones.

64
00:05:36,333 --> 00:05:44,233
They identified extensive healing and argued that the person
had lived for at least another six to nine years

65
00:05:44,233 --> 00:05:47,633
after the injury, which probably happened in childhood.

66
00:05:47,633 --> 00:05:52,667
Their interpretation, published in Nature in twenty twenty-
two, was deliberate surgical amputation.

67
00:05:52,667 --> 00:05:57,700
If correct, that places a successful operation astonishingly
far back in time.

68
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:04,767
But the bones do not preserve the operator's name, the
instrument, or a recipe for managing pain.

69
00:06:04,767 --> 00:06:11,033
And there is a twist the headline often leaves out: other
specialists challenged the diagnosis.

70
00:06:11,033 --> 00:06:17,700
In a published response, orthopaedic surgeons proposed a
different explanation involving a severe fracture during
growth.

71
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:22,733
They argued that the skeletal changes did not require a
deliberate operation.

72
00:06:22,733 --> 00:06:25,300
The original researchers defended their conclusion.

73
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:35,233
They said the location and form of the damage did not match
that proposed fracture, and maintained that amputation best
explained what they observed.

74
00:06:35,233 --> 00:06:40,667
Here is the difficult part. A bone can show that an injury
healed.

75
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:48,567
Reconstructing exactly what caused it is another question.
Surviving a terrible injury and proving an operation are
different claims.

76
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:56,467
We also cannot fill the missing details with a magical leaf,
a miracle ointment, or an exact survival percentage.

77
00:06:56,467 --> 00:07:02,300
A dramatic possibility is still a possibility that has to
answer to the bones.

78
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:10,200
And none of this makes a broken leg safe. It gives us a more
interesting answer than instant doom.

79
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:14,833
Injury, survival, skill, and help could exist in the same
world.

80
00:07:14,833 --> 00:07:22,300
Back at our imaginary camp, there is no guarantee of a happy
ending. A severe injury could kill.

81
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:29,367
An injury could heal badly. Someone might survive with
lasting pain or a different way of moving.

82
00:07:29,367 --> 00:07:37,267
But look at what changed after that first crack. Other
people found him. Shelter became reachable. Food kept
arriving.

83
00:07:37,267 --> 00:07:43,100
The world did not become gentle. He stopped facing every
part of it alone.

84
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:48,533
Would a broken leg end your life thirty thousand years ago?
It could.

85
00:07:48,533 --> 00:07:53,167
But healed injuries show that it did not always do so.

86
00:07:53,167 --> 00:07:57,800
The outcome depended on the injury and the circumstances
around it.

87
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:04,867
Our traveller notices a companion bringing another meal. At
the start, we were staring at his leg.

88
00:08:04,867 --> 00:08:10,700
By the end, we should also be looking at the person who came
back.

89
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:17,367
Then he points toward the path and offers a helpful
suggestion. Apparently, there is a shortcut.

90
00:08:17,367 --> 00:08:22,400
His companion looks at him. He decides not to finish that
sentence.
